{"title":"Parallel postulate","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>In <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometry\" title=\"Geometry\">geometry</a>, the parallel postulate is the fifth postulate in Euclid's Elements and a distinctive axiom in Euclidean geometry. It states that, in two-dimensional geometry:\n</p><p>If a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Line_segment\" title=\"Line segment\">line segment</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Intersect\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Intersect\">intersects</a> two straight <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Line\" title=\"Line\">lines</a> forming two <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Interior_angle\" title=\"Interior angle\">interior angles</a> on the same side that are less than two <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Right_angle\" title=\"Right angle\">right angles</a>, then the two <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Line\" title=\"Line\">lines</a>, if extended indefinitely, meet on that side on which the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Angle\" title=\"Angle\">angles</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sum\" title=\"Sum\">sum</a> to less than two <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Right_angle\" title=\"Right angle\">right angles</a>.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Parallel_postulate&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018102615\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.008 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.012 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 7/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 295/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 0/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,temp